    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_111'), 'Two ways to write the next task for the same agent:

Task A:
  Fix the refund migration so the build passes. Make it robust and keep
  the code clean. Tell me when you are done.

Task B:
  Fix db/migrations/20261019_003_enrollment_refund_deadline.sql so that existing
  enrollments get refund_deadline = created_at + interval ''7 days''.
  Done when  npm run agent:check  exits 0 (it fails on deleted or renamed
  tests, changed applied migrations, added .skip/.only, or a failing build).
  Budget: 20 turns; if you are not done by then, stop and report.

Statement: "Only Task B gives the agent a stop condition that it can verify by running a command, and that it cannot meet by deleting the migration or the tests."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Task A ends in the agent''s own judgement ("robust", "clean", "tell me when you are done"); the only observable signal in it is "the build passes", which, as the incident showed, can be reached by deleting guard files. Task B names the behavior, a command that decides done, and a check that fails on exactly the shortcuts the agent took last time, plus a budget so that being stuck is reported. The misconception is that a longer or more polite prompt makes an agent careful; what changes the loop is the observation that means "done".', 2, 5
