---
name: add-course-seed
description: Seed helper.
---
(35 lines: where course rows go in db/seeds, how to pick the next order index, run npm run seed:check)

Agent session, task "Add the new Rust Foundations course to the catalog":
> Skills available: add-course-seed - Seed helper.
> Editing db/seeds/catalog.sql ...
(SKILL.md is never opened; the new row reuses order index 12, which another course already has)

The team changes only the description:
description: Adds a course to the CodeDev catalog seed (row, next free order index, seed check). Use when a task adds, renames or removes a course in the catalog.

Statement: "The new description makes it more likely that the agent opens this skill for the Rust course task, because the name and description are what the agent sees when it decides whether a skill fits the task."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Agent Skills are loaded in steps: at the start the agent only sees each skill''s name and description, and it reads the body of SKILL.md when it judges that the skill fits the task. "Seed helper." says neither what the skill does nor when to use it, so nothing connects it to "add a course to the catalog". The new description names the task and the trigger (Use when ...), which is the signal the agent decides on. It is "more likely", not guaranteed: the way to verify is to rerun the same task and check in the session that SKILL.md was read and npm run seed:check was run. The misconception is that an agent reads every skill in full, so the description does not matter.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_211') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
