WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_311'), 'The agent was started from a terminal that still had a production DATABASE_URL from a report earlier that morning:

$ echo $DATABASE_URL
postgres://app:***@db.prod.codedev.internal:5432/codedev

Agent session (task: "add a level column to courses"):
14:02:11 $ npm run migrate
Connected to db.prod.codedev.internal:5432/codedev
Applied 20261019_add_course_level.sql

package.json:
"test": "vitest run",
"migrate": "node scripts/migrate.mjs"

tests/setup.ts (runs before every test file):
await migrate(process.env.DATABASE_URL);
await truncateTables(process.env.DATABASE_URL, ["enrollments", "progress"]);

The PR that closes the incident adds "npm run migrate" to the agent''s deny list and changes nothing else.

Statement: "After this PR, an agent started from the same terminal cannot change the production database with the commands it is still allowed to run."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The deny list blocks one spelling of one command, but the privilege that caused the incident is the production credential in the agent''s environment, and every command inherits it. npm test is still allowed, and tests/setup.ts migrates and then truncates the enrollments and progress tables of whatever DATABASE_URL points to - here production. node scripts/migrate.mjs and npx prisma migrate deploy would also get through. The fix that removes the whole class is to start agents in an environment that holds no production credentials (a sandbox or container with a test database, a separate database user without DDL rights) and to keep the command policy as a second layer. The misconception is that denying the command that bit you once removes the risk; least privilege is about what the agent can reach, not about the names of commands.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_311') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', false),
        ('False', true)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);
WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_311'), 'The first version of the harness checked commands like this, then ran the approved string with bash -c:

const SAFE = ["npm test", "npm run lint", "npm run typecheck"];
const isSafe = (cmd: string) => SAFE.some((s) => cmd.startsWith(s));

