UPDATE challenge.challenges SET quiz_display_count = NULL WHERE id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_321');
WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_321'), 'Task: "Fix the level filter on the catalog page". Allowed paths: src/catalog/**, tests/catalog/**.

$ git diff --name-status main...agent/catalog-level
M	src/catalog/filterCourses.ts
A	tests/catalog/filterCourses.test.ts
R100	db/migrations/20261003_add_course_level.sql	src/catalog/legacy/20261003_add_course_level.sql

The first version of the scope check looked only at the new path of each change and printed:
scope: ok (3 changes, all inside allowed paths)

Statement: "The check is right: every file this diff touches ends up under src/catalog or tests/catalog, so the migrations folder is unchanged."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. R100 means the file was moved without edits, and a move changes two paths: it adds src/catalog/legacy/20261003_add_course_level.sql and removes db/migrations/20261003_add_course_level.sql. The migration runner reads db/migrations, so on the next fresh database (a new developer, CI, a preview environment) the level column is never created. A scope check must check both the old and the new path of a rename, and deletions too: removing a file is a change to that path. The misconception is that a rename is a change to the destination only.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_321') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', false),
        ('False', true)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);
WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_321'), 'Task: "Add a level filter helper". Allowed paths in the task file: src/catalog/*.ts, tests/catalog/*.test.ts.

$ git diff --name-status main...agent/level-helper
M	src/catalog/filterCourses.ts
A	src/catalog/filters/byLevel.ts
A	tests/catalog/filters/byLevel.test.ts

The harness uses the glob rules of this lesson: "*" matches characters inside one path segment, "**" matches whole segments.

Statement: "The scope check reports src/catalog/filters/byLevel.ts and tests/catalog/filters/byLevel.test.ts as outside the allowed paths."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. In src/catalog/*.ts the * can only match characters inside one segment, so it matches src/catalog/filterCourses.ts but not src/catalog/filters/byLevel.ts, whose name sits one folder deeper; the same holds for the test. The agent did nothing dangerous here - it created a sub-folder - which is exactly why the check is useful: either the task''s scope was too narrow (write src/catalog/**/*.ts) or the agent restructured more than it was asked to, and a person decides which. The misconception is that * means "everything below this folder".', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_321') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', true),
        ('False', false)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);

-- challenge chal_coding_acp_322 (mini_coding)
INSERT INTO challenge.challenges (public_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, name, title, description, status, difficulty, type, points, is_published, is_deleted)
VALUES ('chal_coding_acp_322', (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'agentic-coding-practice'), (SELECT id FROM course.module WHERE course_id = (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'agentic-coding-practice') AND order_index = 3 AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), (SELECT id FROM course.lesson WHERE content_source = 'lesson-acp-3-2' AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), 'agent-change-scope-check', 'Check the agent''s diff against its allowed paths', 'After the coding agent finishes a task, the CodeDev harness compares the changed files with the paths the task allows and the paths no task may touch (migrations, CI config, lockfiles unless the task allows them, secrets), and refuses to open the PR while there are violations. Write matchGlob(pattern, path) and checkScope(changes, allowed, forbidden).

Paths to check: added, modified and deleted changes give their path; a renamed change gives two, its old path (from) as change "renamed-from" and its new path (path) as "renamed-to". For each path the first of these that applies is a violation, otherwise the path is fine: (1) "invalid path": empty, contains "\", or has a segment (between "/") that is empty, "." or ".." (so a leading or trailing "/" is invalid); (2) "forbidden: <pattern>": the first entry of forbidden, in list order, whose pattern matches - skipping an entry with unlessAllowed true when the path matches an allowed pattern; (3) "outside allowed paths": no allowed pattern matches.

Return { ok, checked, violations }: checked = number of paths checked, ok = no violations, violations = [{ path, change, reason }] sorted by path, then by change (strings compared with < and >).

matchGlob matches the whole path. Braces first: "{" starts a group that ends at the next "}"; the text between is split on "," and one alternative must match; several groups give every combination; an alternative may be empty; a "{" with no "}" after it is a plain character. Then pattern and path are split on "/". A segment that is exactly "**" matches zero or more whole path segments. In other segments "*" matches any run of characters (also none) and "?" exactly one, both inside that segment only, and they also match a leading "."; every other character matches itself, case-sensitive (".", "(", ")", "[", "]", "+" are plain). So "*.pem" does not match "certs/dev.pem" and "**/*.pem" does.

Input: { allowed, forbidden: [{ pattern, unlessAllowed }], changes: [{ status: "added" | "modified" | "deleted", path } | { status: "renamed", from, path }] }. The test runner prints the report as JSON.', 'open', 'medium'::difficulty_question, 'mini_coding', 30, true, false)
