    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_421'), 'app/studio/OutlineButton.tsx (written by the agent):
"use client";
export function OutlineButton({ title }: { title: string }) {
  async function suggest() {
    const res = await fetch("https://api.ai-vendor.test/v1/outline", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_AI_API_KEY}` },
      body: JSON.stringify({ topic: title, sections: 3 }),
    });
    // ...
  }
  return <button onClick={suggest}>Suggest an outline</button>;
}

Terminal, after the production build:
$ grep -rl "sk-fake-4f9a" .next/static
.next/static/chunks/app/studio/page-8c41d2.js

Statement: "Anyone who opens the studio can copy the key from this chunk, so moving the call to a server route is not enough on its own: the key must also be revoked and replaced."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. A file under .next/static is served to every browser that loads the page; the grep shows the build wrote the key''s value into it, so anyone can find it in DevTools > Sources or in the network response, and copies of that chunk may sit in browser caches, CDNs and screenshots. Moving the call behind a server route stops new builds from shipping it, but the old value has already been published: treat it as leaked, revoke it at the vendor, issue a new key that only the server reads, and check the vendor''s usage log for calls you did not make. The misconception is that deleting the line removes the leak.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_acp_421') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
